#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161902 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162089 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e82e8b16-51a8-59c8-9ed1-51619335de97" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Pseudoinverse of a Matrix Given Its Singular Value Decomposition"}] :question/id #uuid "e0a12130-2908-58fd-a8e0-91083fa30e96" :question/math-academy-id "e-9828" :question/problem "![](images/39/399738d7c4da59c73c954cfd41d8c76fc93ad0351fc8f2854ad33796aa2379df.svg)\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A=U \\Sigma V^T$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ find the pseudoinverse $A^+.$" :question/worked-solution "If $A=U \\Sigma V ^T$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ then the pseudoinverse of $A$ is given by\n\n$$\nV \\, \\Sigma^+ \\, U^T,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\Sigma^+$ is the pseudoinverse of $\\Sigma.$ \n\nFirst, notice that\n\n![](images/f6/f6703dcf594334ec5ab821bbaac5654de5e3970b0e7981f256a8fd00df31060f.svg)\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{+} & =V\\,{Σ}^{+}\\,{U}^{T} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{25}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{25}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{25}[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161903 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162090 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3460bfd6-8b2f-5a52-891a-4c8b2af5b5e8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Pseudoinverse of a Diagonal Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "64e7f84f-da34-5ae2-b2d1-72373a1d2888" :question/math-academy-id "e-9829" :question/problem "For the matrix \n![](images/1d/1d0733f2c724253c804c596e1d0a1432b8281afa7a14a9dc6702b099ab4795e0.svg) find the pseudoinverse $A^+.$" :question/worked-solution "For an \"almost\" zero $m \\times n$ matrix with non-zero entries \n\n$$\n\\sigma_1, \\: \\sigma_2, \\: \\ldots, \\: \\sigma_r\n$$\n\non the main diagonal, the corresponding pseudoinverse is the \"almost\" zero $n \\times m$ matrix with entries \n\n$$\n{\\sigma_1^{-1}}, \\, {\\sigma_2^{-1}}, \\, \\ldots, \\, {\\sigma_r^{-1}}\n$$\n\non the main diagonal.\n\nTherefore, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{+} & ={[\\begin{aligned}6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]}^{+} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{6}^{-1} & \\,\\,\\,0 & 0 \\\\ \\,\\,0 & \\,{4}^{-1} & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161904 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162091 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ff858037-da99-53e9-acdc-e4963e7d765e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Pseudoinverse of a Matrix Given Its Reduced Singular Value Decomposition"}] :question/id #uuid "66c34c76-6243-50b8-832f-027abb6ab736" :question/math-academy-id "e-9830" :question/problem "![](images/fd/fdc735895573e0d7c1eb7865233dd7b56a38aeeb9c76bba77fa669212262791d.svg)\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A=U_r \\Sigma_r V_r^T$ is a reduced singular value decomposition of $A,$ find the pseudoinverse $A^+.$" :question/worked-solution "If $A=U_r \\Sigma_r V_r ^T$ is a reduced singular value decomposition of $A,$ then the pseudoinverse of $A$ is given by\n\n$$\nV_r \\, \\Sigma_r^+ \\, U_r^T,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\Sigma_r^+$ is the pseudoinverse of $\\Sigma_r.$\n\nFirst, notice that\n\n![](images/d7/d750c1c5030e0d1df7e8b14790e3f189e69346ca392d3d2885ad49db0e66b7e0.svg)\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{+} & ={V}_{r}\\,{Σ}_{r}^{+}\\,{U}_{r}^{T} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{50}[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{50}[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{50}[\\begin{aligned}12 & 16 & 0 \\\\ 9 & 12 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162344 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162089 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e82e8b16-51a8-59c8-9ed1-51619335de97" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Pseudoinverse of a Matrix Given Its Singular Value Decomposition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "065fffc8-3e59-5969-929b-5087533bf93f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{25}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 4 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432586} {:answer/id #uuid "39695dbd-9cb4-567f-a279-8277626c4d7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{16}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432588} {:answer/id #uuid "597aa326-f1e8-5701-9c20-5031aee7b048" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{16}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 4 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432590} {:answer/id #uuid "fcd31e64-a634-576e-a8e8-c7e2bca6ed34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{25}[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432594} {:answer/id #uuid "ffb3d5ad-4c33-5033-8c68-0522a4f523a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{25}[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432595}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "065fffc8-3e59-5969-929b-5087533bf93f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{25}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 4 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432586} :answer-field/id #uuid "821729eb-93c4-5d09-9aa0-cfa962c2ddca" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159266}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "989a4485-e5eb-5b78-be60-afb9e4f1a969" :question/math-academy-id "q-136972" :question/problem "$$\nU=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & 0 & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ find the pseudoinverse ${A}^{+}.$" :question/worked-solution "If $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ then the pseudoinverse of $A$ is given by\n\n$$\nV\\,{Σ}^{+}\\,{U}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere ${Σ}^{+}$ is the pseudoinverse of $Σ.$\n\nFirst, notice that\n\n$$\nV=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{Σ}^{+}=\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{U}^{T}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\sqrt{5} \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{+} & =V\\,{Σ}^{+}\\,{U}^{T} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\sqrt{5} \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{25}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\sqrt{5} \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{25}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\sqrt{5} \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{25}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 4 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162348 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162091 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ff858037-da99-53e9-acdc-e4963e7d765e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Pseudoinverse of a Matrix Given Its Reduced Singular Value Decomposition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e62f475-a411-5471-9109-4f0776a4b27e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{144}[\\begin{aligned}5 & 6 & -13 \\\\ -13 & 6 & 5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371757} {:answer/id #uuid "574d747d-2aa6-5905-8aa7-23b220e6387d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{72}[\\begin{aligned}-11 & 8 & 7 \\\\ 7 & 8 & -11\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371758} {:answer/id #uuid "03f17a11-c9e6-5b87-859e-5ff94559f3f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{72}[\\begin{aligned}-11 & 7 \\\\ 8 & 8 \\\\ 7 & -11\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371759} {:answer/id #uuid "8cffa160-d3a3-5a20-bf3c-7361ce0636c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{72}[\\begin{aligned}7 & 8 & -11 \\\\ -11 & 8 & 7\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371760} {:answer/id #uuid "41bc4d94-8584-56fc-a285-e1f7e9c02900" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{144}[\\begin{aligned}5 & -13 \\\\ 6 & 6 \\\\ -13 & 5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371761}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8cffa160-d3a3-5a20-bf3c-7361ce0636c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{72}[\\begin{aligned}7 & 8 & -11 \\\\ -11 & 8 & 7\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371760} :answer-field/id #uuid "ef734dd1-af99-5319-9529-058b09954f82" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371756}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e9a9aba1-e21b-5ed5-95bf-a323e7389c16" :question/math-academy-id "q-136992" :question/problem "$$\n{U}_{r}=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{4}{3\\sqrt{2}} & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}],\\,{Σ}_{r}=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}],\\,{V}_{r}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A={U}_{r}{Σ}_{r}{V}_{r}^{T}$ is a reduced singular value decomposition of $A,$ find the pseudoinverse ${A}^{+}.$" :question/worked-solution "If $A={U}_{r}{Σ}_{r}{V}_{r}^{T}$ is a reduced singular value decomposition of $A,$ then the pseudoinverse of $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n{V}_{r}\\,{Σ}_{r}^{+}\\,{U}_{r}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere ${Σ}_{r}^{+}$ is the pseudoinverse of ${Σ}_{r}.$\n\nFirst, notice that\n\n$$\n{V}_{r}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{Σ}_{r}^{+}=\\frac{1}{24}[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{aligned}],\\,{U}_{r}^{T}=\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 4 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{+} & ={V}_{r}\\,{Σ}_{r}^{+}\\,{U}_{r}^{T} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{24}[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 4 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{144}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 & 4 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{144}[\\begin{aligned}4 & -6 \\\\ 4 & 6\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 & 4 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{144}[\\begin{aligned}14 & 16 & -22 \\\\ -22 & 16 & 14\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{72}[\\begin{aligned}7 & 8 & -11 \\\\ -11 & 8 & 7\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371769 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162090 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3460bfd6-8b2f-5a52-891a-4c8b2af5b5e8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Pseudoinverse of a Diagonal Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a1174f7d-b141-5713-b4de-b75c8c38e687" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{12}[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371771} {:answer/id #uuid "1cc78ed8-c7f0-5a41-9e00-adfce49b41d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{12}[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371772} {:answer/id #uuid "2050f3bd-41a7-5866-b243-a040316444c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{12}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371773} {:answer/id #uuid "6191e9c0-c24f-5a04-910e-de5e0d0ab900" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{12}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371774} {:answer/id #uuid "81b5f811-6be5-5b3d-aa46-eeac01b5d282" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{12}[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371775}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2050f3bd-41a7-5866-b243-a040316444c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{12}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371773} :answer-field/id #uuid "1f429e61-6e5a-5466-8a5d-edfebd974ab3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371770}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c9575169-4ae5-57f9-8023-f0f8f01a29aa" :question/math-academy-id "q-138488" :question/problem "For the matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}],$ find the pseudoinverse ${A}^{+}.$" :question/worked-solution "For an \"almost\" zero $m\\times n$ matrix with non-zero entries \n\n$$\n{\\sigma }_{1},\\,{\\sigma }_{2},\\,…,\\,{\\sigma }_{r}\n$$\n\non the main diagonal, the corresponding pseudoinverse is the \"almost\" zero $n\\times m$ matrix with entries \n\n$$\n{\\sigma }_{1}^{-1},\\,{\\sigma }_{2}^{-1},\\,…,\\,{\\sigma }_{r}^{-1}\n$$\n\non the main diagonal.\n\nTherefore, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{+} & ={[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}]}^{+} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{4}^{-1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {3}^{-1} \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371776 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162090 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3460bfd6-8b2f-5a52-891a-4c8b2af5b5e8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Pseudoinverse of a Diagonal Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "114670ea-3d2e-5380-9b00-1c67aa39ab82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{18}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371778} {:answer/id #uuid "6e3ac17f-0d22-5408-a180-7e14809bff32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371779} {:answer/id #uuid "b568db7a-6de2-5e3d-b28d-db1f4b481b41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{18}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371780} {:answer/id #uuid "dbddc463-66e3-5f58-bc72-b9be3864f3c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371781} {:answer/id #uuid "5f33101c-deb4-5f20-b0e3-b5e0e01119c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{9}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371782}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "114670ea-3d2e-5380-9b00-1c67aa39ab82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{18}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371778} :answer-field/id #uuid "0514fb02-a78d-5d05-9503-00c65f87842e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371777}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "040fccf0-1600-5a82-818e-9b2c34bf128b" :question/math-academy-id "q-138493" :question/problem "For the matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -9 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],$ find the pseudoinverse ${A}^{+}.$" :question/worked-solution "For an \"almost\" zero $m\\times n$ matrix with non-zero entries \n\n$$\n{\\sigma }_{1},\\,{\\sigma }_{2},\\,…,\\,{\\sigma }_{r}\n$$\n\non the main diagonal, the corresponding pseudoinverse is the \"almost\" zero $n\\times m$ matrix with entries \n\n$$\n{\\sigma }_{1}^{-1},\\,{\\sigma }_{2}^{-1},\\,…,\\,{\\sigma }_{r}^{-1}\n$$\n\non the main diagonal.\n\nTherefore, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{+} & ={[\\begin{aligned}6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -9 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]}^{+} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{6}^{-1} & \\,\\,\\,0 & 0 \\\\ \\,\\,0 & \\,(-9)^{-1} & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{18}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432597 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162089 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e82e8b16-51a8-59c8-9ed1-51619335de97" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Pseudoinverse of a Matrix Given Its Singular Value Decomposition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "38c000fb-0f6e-5949-8db6-c4c5d28b3248" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432587} {:answer/id #uuid "3fe77835-4c02-5a54-960e-f2c670d5fae4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432589} {:answer/id #uuid "8e69fde6-984a-5a8b-8dd0-63b58423f006" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432591} {:answer/id #uuid "afeacb94-4e5b-58cc-be9f-c0b7884171f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432592} {:answer/id #uuid "c0338917-af25-5f02-ba7d-b2159df253f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432593}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8e69fde6-984a-5a8b-8dd0-63b58423f006" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432591} :answer-field/id #uuid "2881387b-754e-56f3-834a-ac7d858e2ecf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432596}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3fcee73d-521b-5fd0-a9c6-c7b6d8e05cce" :question/math-academy-id "q-138516" :question/problem "$$\nU=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ find the pseudoinverse ${A}^{+}.$" :question/worked-solution "If $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ then the pseudoinverse of $A$ is given by\n\n$$\nV\\,{Σ}^{+}\\,{U}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere ${Σ}^{+}$ is the pseudoinverse of $Σ.$\n\nFirst, notice that\n\n$$\nV=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{Σ}^{+}=\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{U}^{T}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{+} & =V\\,{Σ}^{+}\\,{U}^{T} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388552 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162091 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ff858037-da99-53e9-acdc-e4963e7d765e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Pseudoinverse of a Matrix Given Its Reduced Singular Value Decomposition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "95aa3141-e8ca-53cd-80c2-927fdca976aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{50}[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 0 & -7\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388554} {:answer/id #uuid "b5fdec83-023c-50aa-a518-6e4e8482250b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{50}[\\begin{aligned}-7 & 0 & 6 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 6\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388555} {:answer/id #uuid "7520040e-4dcb-567d-99b1-b572de819cfa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{50}[\\begin{aligned}6 & 0 & -7 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 6\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388556} {:answer/id #uuid "f7e39201-ff92-5aa6-bf59-03f275ce9b4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{50}[\\begin{aligned}-7 & 0 & 6 \\\\ -6 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388557} {:answer/id #uuid "80f0b7a6-f32e-5a85-8574-16e142f64353" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{50}[\\begin{aligned}0 & -7 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 6 & -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388558}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f7e39201-ff92-5aa6-bf59-03f275ce9b4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{50}[\\begin{aligned}-7 & 0 & 6 \\\\ -6 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388557} :answer-field/id #uuid "ff1f002b-652d-5333-8455-842c9d746458" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388553}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "0806e0ef-e656-5bbb-b90b-e4ef2729a281" :question/math-academy-id "q-138621" :question/problem "$$\n{U}_{r}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}],\\,{Σ}_{r}=[\\begin{aligned}10 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}],\\,{V}_{r}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A={U}_{r}{Σ}_{r}{V}_{r}^{T}$ is a reduced singular value decomposition of $A,$ find the pseudoinverse ${A}^{+}.$" :question/worked-solution "If $A={U}_{r}{Σ}_{r}{V}_{r}^{T}$ is a reduced singular value decomposition of $A,$ then the pseudoinverse of $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n{V}_{r}\\,{Σ}_{r}^{+}\\,{U}_{r}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere ${Σ}_{r}^{+}$ is the pseudoinverse of ${Σ}_{r}.$\n\nFirst, notice that\n\n$$\n{V}_{r}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{Σ}_{r}^{+}=\\frac{1}{10}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{U}_{r}^{T}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{+} & ={V}_{r}\\,{Σ}_{r}^{+}\\,{U}_{r}^{T} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{10}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{50}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{50}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{50}[\\begin{aligned}-7 & 0 & 6 \\\\ -6 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
